          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "You should consult with a healthcare professional for treatment guidance, and seek medical advice if your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained (diagnostic). While an automated system initially assessed your symptoms as routine, the presence of fever and back pain (flank pain) are features that move the presentation out of the routine category, according to the \"Urinary tract infection\" document (diagnostic). The research specialist advises you should seek immediate medical attention for your symptoms (research).\n\nYour description of burning or pain on urination, temperature (fever), and back pain (flank pain) is described in the document \"Urinary tract infection\" as characteristic of an upper tract infection (diagnostic).\n\nRegarding treatment, urine culture is generally reserved for cases like suspected pyelonephritis (a type of kidney infection), complicated infections, or recurrent infections, as it helps guide management, according to guidance from the Infectious diseases and urology society (educational summary) (research). For treatment of uncomplicated urinary tract infections, which can sometimes escalate to pyelonephritis if untreated, guidelines suggest short-course therapy with an agent that concentrates in urine and has minimal effect on gut flora (research). Nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (where local resistance is low), and fosfomycin are commonly described as first-line options. Fluoroquinolones are generally reserved due to serious side effects and their importance in other infections. Beta-lactams are alternative options but may have somewhat lower efficacy. The choice of antibiotic is driven by local resistance patterns (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence, Infectious diseases and urology society guidance, research).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
